{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that translates Python code to GNU C++11 code. The code should reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Translate the following Python 3 code to GNU C++11 code. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your translated code should have the exact same functionality as the given code, meaning that it should have the same output for any given input.\nPrint your translated code and also the confidence score you give to your translation. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<translated_code>\n<your_translated_code>\n</translated_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the original Python 3 code you have to translate:\n<original_code>\nimport sys\nimport itertools\n\nvar_for_sublime = False\n\nif var_for_sublime:\n    fin = open('input.txt', 'r')\n    fout = open('output.txt', 'w')\n    sys.stdout = fout\n    def give_string():\n        return fin.readline().strip()\nelse:\n    fin = sys.stdin\n    fout = sys.stdout\n    def give_string():\n        return fin.buffer.readline().strip()\n\ndef give_list():\n    return list(map(int, give_string().split()))\ndef give_int():\n    return int(give_string())\n\npairings = [\n    (0, 0),  # 1: a1 with b1\n    (0, 2),  # 2: a1 with b3\n    (1, 1),  # 3: a2 with b2\n    (1, 0),  # 4: a2 with b1\n    (2, 2),  # 5: a3 with b3\n    (2, 1)   # 6: a3 with b2\n]\n\ndef solve():\n    n = give_int()\n    a = give_list()  # [a1, a2, a3]\n    b = give_list()  # [b1, b2, b3]\n    mx = min(a[0], b[1]) + min(a[1], b[2]) + min(a[2], b[0])\n    temp = 0\n    for per in itertools.permutations([0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]):\n        ta = a[:]\n        tb = b[:]\n        tmx = 0\n        for idx in per:\n            ai, bi = pairings[idx]\n            t1 = min(ta[ai], tb[bi])\n            ta[ai] -= t1\n            tb[bi] -= t1\n            tmx += t1\n        temp = max(temp, tmx)\n    print(n - temp, end=' ')\n    print(mx)\n\ndef Hey_Yashwant():\n    try:\n        t = 1\n        for i in range(t):\n            solve()\n    finally:\n        fin.close()\n        fout.close()\n        sys.stdout = original_stdout\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    original_stdout = sys.stdout\n    Hey_Yashwant()\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<translated_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <array>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\n// Pairings: (ai, bi) indices\nconst array<pair<int,int>, 6> pairings = {{\n    {0, 0}, // 1: a1 with b1\n    {0, 2}, // 2: a1 with b3\n    {1, 1}, // 3: a2 with b2\n    {1, 0}, // 4: a2 with b1\n    {2, 2}, // 5: a3 with b3\n    {2, 1}  // 6: a3 with b2\n}};\n\nvoid solve() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    array<int,3> a, b;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) cin >> a[i];\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) cin >> b[i];\n\n    // mx = min(a[0], b[1]) + min(a[1], b[2]) + min(a[2], b[0])\n    int mx = min(a[0], b[1]) + min(a[1], b[2]) + min(a[2], b[0]);\n\n    int temp = 0;\n    vector<int> per = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    do {\n        array<int,3> ta = a;\n        array<int,3> tb = b;\n        int tmx = 0;\n        for (int idx : per) {\n            int ai = pairings[idx].first;\n            int bi = pairings[idx].second;\n            int t1 = min(ta[ai], tb[bi]);\n            ta[ai] -= t1;\n            tb[bi] -= t1;\n            tmx += t1;\n        }\n        temp = max(temp, tmx);\n    } while (next_permutation(per.begin(), per.end()));\n\n    cout << n - temp << ' ' << mx << endl;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    // Only one test case in this problem\n    solve();\n    return 0;\n}\n</translated_code>\n<confidence>\n1.0\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-6.6756979322235566e-06, 0.0, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.0001642